## 10. Power Law Investing

### The Framework

Gerstner abandoned traditional VC math (3-5x returns) for **power law investing**:

> *"I spent my early career trying to find companies that would return 3-5x. Then I realized: that math doesn't work. The only math that works in venture is power law math. One company returns the fund. Everything else is noise."*

### The Power Law Math

| Investment | Outcome | Multiple | Return |
|-----------|---------|----------|--------|
| Snowflake | Home run | 100x+ | Returns entire fund |
| Other 10–14 investments | Average | 3–10x | Mixed |
| Write-offs | Losses | 0x | Expected |

> *"If you own 20 companies and each one is 5% of the fund, you're not concentrated — you're diversified into mediocrity. Power law says the outcome of the top 1-2 positions determines your entire return. Size accordingly."*

### The Entry Point: Series B/C Handoff

Altimeter explicitly **avoids Seed and Series A**:

| Stage | Avoid | Reason |
|-------|-------|--------|
| **Seed** | ❌ | No PMF, no revenue, no signal |
| **Series A** | ❌ | PMF unproven, team still forming |
| **Series B/C** | ✅ | PMF established, revenue visible, scaling begins |

> *"We don't do Seed or Series A. We enter at the B/C handoff — after PMF is proven, when the company is ready to scale. That's when you can size up meaningfully. That's when the power law starts to be calculable."*

### The "100x Targeting" Method

Instead of projecting 3-5x on 20 companies, Gerstner's team:

1. Screens 1,000+ companies
2. Deep dives on 50–100
3. Invests in 12–15 per fund
4. Each position sized to return the fund (5–15% ownership target)
5. Top 3 positions = 50–70% of fund capital

> *"We want to own 10-15% of a company at Series B/C. That's how you get the power law working for you, not against you. Small ownership = small returns. Period."*

### Portfolio Construction Rules

1. **12–15 positions maximum** per fund
2. **Top 3 positions** = 50-70% of capital
3. **Minimum position** = can return 3x fund (otherwise don't invest)
4. **No "spray and pray"** — Tiger Global's model is explicitly rejected
5. **Stage discipline** — Series B/C only (public markets are a different strategy)

