## 12. Memory Wall

### The Physics Problem

The **memory wall** is the single most important technical bottleneck in AI computing:

> *"The memory wall is the fundamental physical constraint on AI progress. Moving data between memory and compute takes 100-1000x more energy than the compute itself. We have entered the era where data movement, not computation, is the binding constraint."*

### The Von Neumann Bottleneck

```
Traditional Architecture:
  
  CPU/GPU  ←→  DRAM/HBM  ←→  SSD/Storage
  (compute)     (memory)       (persistent)
  
  Problem: Bus between compute and memory is the bottleneck
  Data moves at ~1TB/s (HBM) but compute needs 10-100TB/s+
  Energy cost per bit moved = 100-1000x energy cost of compute
```

### Why It Matters for AI

| Model | Parameters | Compute Needed | Memory BW Needed | Wall Impact |
|-------|-----------|----------------|-----------------|-------------|
| GPT-3 | 175B | ~3.1×10²³ FLOPs | ~900 GB/s | High |
| GPT-4 | ~1.8T (est.) | ~2×10²⁵ FLOPs | ~3 TB/s | Very High |
| Future models (2026+) | 10T+ | ~10²⁶ FLOPs | ~10 TB/s+ | Critical |

> *"Nvidia's H100 has 80GB of HBM3 memory with ~3TB/s bandwidth. Sounds fast. It's not. The compute elements can process data 10-100x faster than the memory can feed them. Every inference call is memory-bound, not compute-bound."*

### Cerebras's Solution: Wafer-Scale + SRAM

Cerebras solves the memory wall by putting **SRAM directly adjacent to every compute element on a single wafer-scale chip**:

```
Cerebras WSE-3:

┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ┌───┐ ┌───┐ ┌───┐ ┌───┐ ┌───┐ ┌───┐  │
│  │SRAM│ │SRAM│ │SRAM│ │SRAM│ │SRAM│ │SRAM│  │
│  │+Core│ │+Core│ │+Core│ │+Core│ │+Core│ │+Core│  │
│  └───┘ └───┘ └───┘ └───┘ └───┘ └───┘  │
│  ┌───┐ ┌───┐ ┌───┐ ┌───┐ ┌───┐ ┌───┐  │
│  │SRAM│ │SRAM│ │SRAM│ │SRAM│ │SRAM│ │SRAM│  │
│  │+Core│ │+Core│ │+Core│ │+Core│ │+Core│ │+Core│  │
│  └───┘ └───┘ └───┘ └───┘ └───┘ └───┘  │
│  ... (850,000 cores total)              │
│  ... (44GB SRAM on-chip)                │
└─────────────────────────────────────┘

Result: All data movement is on-chip (pJ/bit, not nJ/bit)
No HBM, no external memory access, no Von Neumann bottleneck
```

**Key Metrics:**
- 15–18x faster inference for transformer models
- 100–1000x less energy per inference
- No external memory bandwidth bottleneck
- Scales linearly with wafer size

### Architectural Implications

> *"The memory wall is not a 2-3 year problem. It's a decade-long structural challenge. Every AI chip company is trying to solve it with bigger HBM stacks, tighter packaging, or on-chip SRAM. Cerebras is the only company I've seen that has a real, physical, architectural solution."*

### Other Approaches Gerstner Tracks

1. **Nvidia**: HBM4 (2026), NVLink for GPU-to-GPU direct memory access
2. **AMD**: Infinity Architecture, chiplets with 3D stacking
3. **Apple**: Unified memory architecture (M-series Ultra)
4. **Groq**: LPU (language processing unit) — SRAM-only, no DRAM
5. **Microsoft / OpenAI**: Custom networking (Maia, etc.)

