## 14. Worst Mistakes

### Mistake #1: ByteDance (the greatest)

**Year:** 2015
**Miss size:** $10B → $350–500B (35-50x)
**Root cause:** Categorization error + China bias

> *"I categorized ByteDance as a Chinese social media company. Wrong. It was an AI recommendation engine with a global TAM. I learned that category errors are the most expensive mistakes in investing."*

**Lesson Applied:**
- AI-first categorization framework
- Global perspective (not US-centric)
- Founder vision over industry taxonomy

### Mistake #2: ZIRP-Era Inflated Follow-On Rounds

**Problem:** During Zero Interest Rate Policy (ZIRP, 2020–2021), Altimeter overpaid in follow-on rounds for companies that were overvalued.

> *"ZIRP was a drug. It made bad companies look good. We bought into companies at 50x revenue that were growing 20%. That math never works. I knew it at the time and did it anyway because FOMO is real."*

**Specific Examples:**
- Inflated cloud/SaaS valuations that corrected in 2022
- Growth-stage rounds at prices never recovered
- Some follow-ons that should have been write-offs

**Lesson Applied:**
- Stricter valuation discipline (20-criteria rubric)
- "Never pay for growth that isn't real" — revenue quality over quantity
- Correlation of growth + unit economics + moat = necessary, not sufficient

### Mistake #3: Underestimating Hyperscaler AI Spend

**Year:** 2022–2023
**Miss:** Underestimated how fast and how much AWS/Azure/GCP would spend on AI infrastructure

> *"In early 2023, I thought hyperscaler AI capex would be maybe $50B. It's going to be $200B+. I was off by 4x. That's how fast this cycle moves. I adjusted, but I should have been bigger, faster."*

**Lesson Applied:**
- Infrastructure bets (Nvidia, CoreWeave, Cerebras) sized up aggressively
- Underestimating AI capex is a systematic bias to correct

### Mistake #4: Hanging onto Falling Knives (LogMeIn/GoTo)

**Problem:** Altimeter took LogMeIn/GoTo private, then spent years trying to turn it around amid massive competitive pressure from Zoom, Teams, Slack.

> *"We should have cut our losses. We threw good money after bad thinking we could turn it around. Some businesses are structurally dying — no amount of management change or capex saves them."*

**Lesson Applied:**
- Kill more things faster
- Capital recycling: a dollar lost in a dying business is a dollar not deployed in a winner
- "Don't try to polish a turd" — Gerstner's phrasing

### Mistake #5: Not Buying More Nvidia in Early 2023

**Year:** Early 2023
**Miss:** Nvidia at $140–150, bought 6-8% position, should have been 15%+

> *"I had the right thesis. I knew Nvidia was the AI infrastructure company. I bought at the bottom. But I didn't size it to match my conviction. I was too diversified. I left billions on the table."*

**Lesson Applied:**
- Sizing > selection: the right idea sized wrong is a wrong investment
- Power law applies to conviction: max conviction = max size

